A||N||6||Zorba||Minor Observations On My 1st V8 Swap!||||10||
Z||000000||Zorba||12-09-2000||04:35 PM||||For those of you thinking about converting your fiero into a v8 powerhouse, I have made a few observations with my first swap.<P>First, it's not difficult to do. I got a little messed up with the wiring (cut one wrong wire) but Archie was quick to help out.<P>Second, if you do it by yourself like I did, it takes A <B>long</B> time to do. You will also make several trips to the local hardware and parts stores. They all know me by first name now!<P>Third, your car has to be in top shape to begin with or, after the conversion, you will be upgrading suspension components.<P>Overall, the conversion is straight forward. I do recommend a hoist though. Makes the job much easier. I am glad I did this conversion and once I work out the bugs out of my rear suspension, I will be able to enjoy driving my new 325hp, carburated fiero. It took me about 3 months from start to finish. I also did my own exhaust system consisting of 90 degree pipes and two TURBO II mufflers. Some basic welding skills is all it takes.<BR>Since the rear springs were 200lb rate/inch<BR>off my 4 cylinder, the rear has been automatically lowered about 2". I need to decide on the best way to lower my front end by the same height. Drop spindles are hardly in stock so I might use a 2" set of lowering springs in the front.<BR><B>(If you have a set to sell, let me know)</B><BR>I am using a CSI Electric Water pump. It also requires sheet metal cutting to be installed. <BR>Anyway...I highly recommend Archie's kit.<BR>My Isuzu 5 speed tranny works nicely with this setup and it feels like a normal car, only with a lot more torque and power for the little I have seen driving around the neighborhood.<P>Bugs to work out before I venture on the freeways:<P>1. Water temp guage does not move. I grounded it on the block to test it and it pegged to the right. Not sure how to fix.<BR>Also wondering if I have enough water pressure inside the block to activate the gauge sensor. No overheating so far during idle (5-10 minutes straight)<P>2. Something is rubbing my right rear dust cover or caliper. Not sure what but it makes an awful noise.<P>3. Rear end dropped 2" with the new engine. I have used a set of 2 1/4" heavy rubber risers to offset this and it seems to have done the job nicely. $5.99/ea At Checkers.<P>4.May need to add a front sway bar in the back but I am not sure until I drive the car a little more. Need suggestions here.<P>So I am enjoying my new "baby" with the v8 in it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/babybraz.gif"> <BR>||||reg||10
Z||000001||RNGCRSH||12-09-2000||05:04 PM||||I dont have a V8 Fiero but after talking with Design One and RCC they both sugest, for a V8 upgrade, using 400 lb springs on the rear to eliminate torque understeer. also you can get drop spindles from Ross at   <A HREF="http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/dropspindles.html" TARGET=_blank>www.iserv.net/~strdream/dropspindles.html</A>  they are 2 inch drop and will work with stock rims and brakes ||||reg||1
Z||000002||pherder||12-09-2000||05:24 PM||||Zorba,<P>     <B>1. Water temp guage does not move. I grounded it on the block to test it and it pegged to the right. Not sure how to fix.</B><P>Need to know what sensor you are using in the block and what temp gauge you are using.<P>The reason ... you will have to use the stock Fiero temp sensor to make the stock Fiero temp gauge work.<P>If you are using a VDO gauge, a VDO sensor is needed.<P>If you are using a stock (for the V8) sensor, it will not work with the stock Fiero gauge (wrong resistance readings.)<P>I don't know where the temp gauge/idiot light <B>SENSOR</B> for the L4 is but for the V6 it is located in the head at the left rear of the engine (looking at the engine from behind the car.)<P>I plan on starting on my V8 swap this winter after getting the Formula on the road (3.2 V6 rebuild).  I will be using a TPI setup since I really enjoy making the wiring work ... <B>NOT!</B>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Congrats on a sucessful swap!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul<P><BR>||||reg||3
Z||000003||Zorba||12-09-2000||09:12 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by pherder:<BR><B>Zorba,<P>     [b]1. Water temp guage does not move. I grounded it  </B><P>I have the fiero stock gauge and sensor on the new v8 block.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Zorba||12-09-2000||09:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RNGCRSH:<BR><B>I dont have a V8 Fiero but after talking with Design One and RCC they both sugest, for a V8 upgrade, using 400 lb springs on the rear to eliminate torque understeer.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Where do I get these springs from? Thanks!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||RNGCRSH||12-09-2000||10:19 PM||||RCC sells a Rear coilover setup that you can configure with different weight springs. <P>they dont have a website but you can reach them M-F at 817-536-3820.<P>also because of them being coilovers you can lower the rear of car without increasing torque understeer. coilovers are the only way to go for lowering the rear of a Fiero IMO ||||reg||1
Z||000006||HYPTNOTiSE||12-10-2000||05:38 PM||||I also have available ground control coilover setups.  You can choose your own spring rate and ride height is adjustable up to 3 inches of lowering.  Very trick looking setup. And they're no harder to install than anyother spring.||||reg||1
